============
LITMUS TESTS
============

Each subdirectory contains litmus tests that are typical to describe the
semantics of respective kernel APIs.
For more information about how to "run" a litmus test or how to generate
a kernel test module based on a litmus test, please see
tools/memory-model/README.


atomic (/atomic directory)
--------------------------

Atomic-RMW+mb__after_atomic-is-stronger-than-acquire.litmus
    Test that an atomic RMW followed by a smp_mb__after_atomic() is
    stronger than a normal acquire: both the read and write parts of
    the RMW are ordered before the subsequential memory accesses.

Atomic-RMW-ops-are-atomic-WRT-atomic_set.litmus
    Test that atomic_set() cannot break the atomicity of atomic RMWs.
    NOTE: Require herd7 7.56 or later which supports "(void)expr".

cmpxchg-fail-ordered-1.litmus
    Demonstrate that a failing cmpxchg() operation acts as a full barrier
    when followed by smp_mb__after_atomic().

cmpxchg-fail-ordered-2.litmus
    Demonstrate that a failing cmpxchg() operation acts as an acquire
    operation when followed by smp_mb__after_atomic().

cmpxchg-fail-unordered-1.litmus
    Demonstrate that a failing cmpxchg() operation does not act as a
    full barrier.

cmpxchg-fail-unordered-2.litmus
    Demonstrate that a failing cmpxchg() operation does not act as an
    acquire operation.


locking (/locking directory)
----------------------------

DCL-broken.litmus
    Demonstrates that double-checked locking needs more than just
    the obvious lock acquisitions and releases.

DCL-fixed.litmus
    Demonstrates corrected double-checked locking that uses
    smp_store_release() and smp_load_acquire() in addition to the
    obvious lock acquisitions and releases.

RM-broken.litmus
    Demonstrates problems with "roach motel" locking, where code is
    freely moved into lock-based critical sections.  This example also
    shows how to use the "filter" clause to discard executions that
    would be excluded by other code not modeled in the litmus test.
    Note also that this "roach motel" optimization is emulated by
    physically moving P1()'s two reads from x under the lock.

    What is a roach motel?  This is from an old advertisement for
    a cockroach trap, much later featured in one of the "Men in
    Black" movies.  "The roaches check in.  They don't check out."

RM-fixed.litmus
    The counterpart to RM-broken.litmus, showing P0()'s two loads from
    x safely outside of the critical section.


RCU (/rcu directory)
--------------------

MP+onceassign+derefonce.litmus (under tools/memory-model/litmus-tests/)
    Demonstrates the use of rcu_assign_pointer() and rcu_dereference() to
    ensure that an RCU reader will not see pre-initialization garbage.

RCU+sync+read.litmus
RCU+sync+free.litmus
    Both the above litmus tests demonstrate the RCU grace period guarantee
    that an RCU read-side critical section can never span a grace period.

RCU-txn+install.litmus
    Demonstrates that the install of an RCU pseudo-transaction
    (include/linux/rcu_txn.h) publishes its record: a reader that finds a
    proxy reads the record initialized.
    NOTE: In all RCU-txn tests, the readers resolve a proxy as
    rcu_txn_dereference() does: they load the slot again, with a
    load-acquire.  The tests of two slots leave the resolve section out,
    to keep herd7 fast.  Their headers say so.

RCU-txn+monotonic+dep.litmus
RCU-txn+monotonic+acquire.litmus
RCU-txn+monotonic+rmb.litmus
    Demonstrate that the slots of one RCU pseudo-transaction resolve old,
    then new.  They never resolve new, then old.  The reader orders its
    two loads in one of three ways:
     - the second load depends on the first (+dep);
     - the reader loads the first slot with a load-acquire, then calls
       rcu_txn_resolve() (+acquire);
     - the reader calls smp_rmb() between its two rcu_txn_dereference()
       (+rmb).

RCU-txn+monotonic+dep+rev.litmus
    As RCU-txn+monotonic+dep, but the writer records the two slots in
    the other order.

RCU-txn+list+step-back.litmus
    Demonstrates that a reader of an rcu_txn_list never skips a node that
    is being inserted.  The reader steps forward onto the node, forward
    again onto its successor, then back.  It comes back to the inserted
    node, not to its predecessor.  The test has the shape of
    RCU-txn+monotonic+dep, with the names of a list.
    NOTE: herd7 took 5 to 7 minutes on each of the +monotonic+dep and
    +list+step-back tests, in testing.  It took about an hour on the 34
    RCU-txn tests.

RCU-txn+sync+free.litmus
RCU-txn+sync+free+section.litmus
    Demonstrate that a reader never reads the record of an RCU
    pseudo-transaction once it is freed after a grace period.  The reader
    reads the record within a resolve section only.  This section
    protects the record by itself (+section).

RCU-txn+publish.litmus
RCU-txn+remove.litmus
    Demonstrate RCU pseudo-transactions end to end, with the writer doing
    every step.  A reader sees the record initialized and a published
    object built.  It never reads a removed object or the record once they
    are freed after a grace period.

RCU-txn+steal.litmus
RCU-txn+steal+reuse.litmus
    Demonstrate that a reader never reads the record of a local
    transaction's descriptor once it is stolen and reused.  In a steal,
    a pool takes its descriptors back without waiting for a grace period,
    after waiting for the resolve sections in flight.  In +reuse, a whole
    second transaction reuses the descriptor, on the same slot.

RCU-txn+recycle.litmus
    Demonstrates that a reader never reads the record of a local
    transaction's descriptor once its CPU reused it, after a grace
    period.

RCU-txn+reuse+monotonic.litmus
    Demonstrates that a transaction on a reused descriptor still resolves
    old, then new.  The selector of the descriptor holds 1 from its
    earlier transaction.  The writer stores 0 to it again before the
    install.

RCU-txn+*+once-*.litmus
RCU-txn+monotonic+unordered.litmus
RCU-txn+remove+nolock.litmus
RCU-txn+remove+nosync.litmus
RCU-txn+sync+free+nosync.litmus
RCU-txn+sync+free+section+noreload.litmus
RCU-txn+steal+noreload.litmus
RCU-txn+steal+nosync.litmus
RCU-txn+steal+reuse+nosync.litmus
RCU-txn+recycle+nosync.litmus
    Error injection into the above RCU-txn tests.  Each variant drops one
    ordering, or one step, that its test relies on.  herd7 then finds the
    outcome possible.  This shows that the model catches the missing
    ordering.
